1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,792
We are now
in the
magnificent Hall
of Mirrors, one
of the most
evocative places

2
00:00:05,792 --> 00:00:11,878
on the entire
tourist route
through the
Grotta del Bue
Marino. In this
section, the

3
00:00:11,878 --> 00:00:18,037
water is usually
so still and
flat that
it becomes
a perfect
reflecting
surface. The

4
00:00:18,037 --> 00:00:24,342
walls and
formations
mirror
themselves,
creating
an upside-down
image
of extraordinary

5
00:00:24,342 --> 00:00:30,794
clarity, giving
the illusion
of a depth far
greater than
it actually is.
It is, however,

6
00:00:30,794 --> 00:00:37,246
only an optical
illusion: at the
deepest point
accessible
to the public,
the water never

7
00:00:37,246 --> 00:00:43,698
exceeds two
metres. The
formation
of these
"mirrors"
depends greatly
on external
weather

8
00:00:43,698 --> 00:00:49,637
conditions. When
a dry wind blows
outside, such
as the
maestrale, the
air remains

9
00:00:49,637 --> 00:00:55,723
stable and the
surface of the
water stays
perfectly still,
allowing the
reflections

10
00:00:55,723 --> 00:01:01,662
to appear in all
their
perfection. If,
on the other
hand, a humid
wind is blowing

11
00:01:01,662 --> 00:01:07,748
outside, such
as the scirocco,
the moisture
penetrates into
the cave. Here
it meets

12
00:01:07,748 --> 00:01:14,347
a naturally
cooler
environment and
a condensation
effect occurs:
the warm, humid
air, upon

13
00:01:14,347 --> 00:01:19,919
contact with the
cooler walls,
produces tiny
water droplets.
In these cases,

14
00:01:19,919 --> 00:01:26,005
inside the Hall
of Mirrors
a kind of very
fine drizzle can
begin, which
ripples the

15
00:01:26,005 --> 00:01:31,504
surface of the
water and makes
the mirror
effect
disappear. The
temperature

16
00:01:31,504 --> 00:01:38,103
inside the cave
is in fact
constant
throughout the
year, ranging
between 16 and
18 degrees

17
00:01:38,103 --> 00:01:43,822
Celsius. The
relative
humidity is very
high and can
reach 99%. It
is precisely

18
00:01:43,822 --> 00:01:49,761
this delicate
balance between
temperature,
humidity, and
external
conditions that

19
00:01:49,761 --> 00:01:56,286
determines the
magic — or the
absence — of the
mirrors. This
hall is also one
of the best

20
00:01:56,286 --> 00:02:01,932
places
to observe the
colours of the
cave. White
is the natural
colour of the

21
00:02:01,932 --> 00:02:08,384
limestone, the
sedimentary rock
of which the
entire mountain
is composed. The
red-orange

22
00:02:08,384 --> 00:02:14,030
is due to the
presence of iron
oxide, a common
element in karst
environments.

23
00:02:14,030 --> 00:02:20,262
The brown tones
are linked
to detrital
deposits: mud,
soil, and
materials
transported

24
00:02:20,262 --> 00:02:26,787
by water over
time. Also
visible on the
walls is a thin
horizontal brown
line, which runs

25
00:02:26,787 --> 00:02:33,166
from the Hall
of Candelabras
to approximately
the halfway
point of the
route. On closer

26
00:02:33,166 --> 00:02:39,252
inspection,
it turns out
to be composed
of oyster
fossils dating
back
approximately

27
00:02:39,252 --> 00:02:45,191
120,000
to 125,000
years. This line
is evidence
of an ancient
sea level,
probably

28
00:02:45,191 --> 00:02:51,130
following
a glacial
period. With the
melting of the
ice, the sea
rose to a height

29
00:02:51,130 --> 00:02:57,582
greater than
today, allowing
oysters
to settle along
the cave walls.
With the
subsequent

30
00:02:57,582 --> 00:03:03,228
lowering of the
sea level, these
organisms became
fossilised,
leaving a trace

31
00:03:03,228 --> 00:03:08,874
that is still
perfectly
visible today.
The same line
marking that
ancient sea

32
00:03:08,874 --> 00:03:15,179
level can also
be observed
along the coasts
of the Gulf
of Orosei, where
it appears as

33
00:03:15,179 --> 00:03:21,411
a clear wave-cut
notch carved
into the rock.
Here, in the
Hall of Mirrors,
we are not

34
00:03:21,411 --> 00:03:27,570
merely observing
reflections:
we are observing
time, climate,
and geological
history

35
00:03:27,570 --> 00:03:28,960
imprinted
in stone.
